<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/21st-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tulip Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2005">2005</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tulip Revolution was a wave of protests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a> in March 2005 that overthrew President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/askar-akayev">Askar Akayev</a>. Triggered by disputed parliamentary elections, it began in the south, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalal-abad">Jalal-Abad</a> and Osh, and culminated in the storming of the presidential offices in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishkek">Bishkek</a> on 24 March. Akayev fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and resigned, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurmanbek-bakiyev">Kurmanbek Bakiyev</a> became president, though he was himself ousted in a further uprising in 2010.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The revolution, also called the First Kyrgyz Revolution, followed parliamentary elections held on 27 February and 13 March 2005. Opponents accused President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/askar-akayev">Askar Akayev</a>, his family and supporters of corruption and authoritarianism. Pro-Akayev candidates did well, but foreign observers criticised the result, and the Organization for Security and Co-operation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> was critical of the government. Akayev himself coined the name Tulip Revolution in a speech in which he warned that no colour revolution should happen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a>, recalling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rose-revolution">Rose Revolution</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-revolution">Orange Revolution</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. The Georgian politician Givi Targamadze, who had consulted Ukrainian opposition leaders, later advised the Kyrgyz opposition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Protests began in the western and southern cities, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalal-abad">Jalal-Abad</a>, Osh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzgen">Uzgen</a>. A bomb exploded in the apartment of the opposition leader Roza Otunbayeva on 3 March; the government denied responsibility. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurmanbek-bakiyev">Kurmanbek Bakiyev</a> joined protesters outside parliament in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishkek">Bishkek</a> on 10 March. On 19 March three thousand people in Bishkek and fifty thousand in Jalal-Abad joined the protests, and by 20 March protesters had taken control of all the large cities of the south. The opposition leaders formed an interim government on 22 March. Akayev dismissed his interior minister and prosecutor general and, on 23 March, deployed riot police.</p><p>On 24 March 2005 Akayev fled with his family, first to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, where President Vladimir Putin offered him exile. Prime Minister Nikolai Tanayev resigned, police melted away or joined the protesters, and imprisoned opposition leaders, including Felix Kulov, were released. Mobs looted stores in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishkek">Bishkek</a> and three people had died. The Supreme Court declared the election results invalid. Bakiyev became acting prime minister and acting president, and Kulov became acting interior minister. Akayev signed his resignation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and the interim parliament ratified it on 11 April.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Presidential elections were held on 10 July 2005. Bakiyev, who had agreed with Kulov that Kulov would be prime minister if he won, took ninety percent of the vote and became president the next day. Western observers praised the conduct of the election but noted irregularities. The Organization for Security and Co-operation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> noted significant shortcomings in the runoff, while observers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-independent-states">Commonwealth of Independent States</a> called it free and fair. Reports also said that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> government and American organisations had funded opposition media. The interim government faced land seizures, unrest and violence before the election, and the alliance of Bakiyev and Kulov lasted until January 2007.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tulip Revolution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Protests overthrew Akayev in the <b>Tulip Revolution</b> of 2005 and his successor Kurmanbek Bakiyev in 2010, after which a parliamentary system was briefly adopted.</blockquote><a href="/state/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a></li></ul></section>
